        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Before You Buy a Treadmill With a Fan: 4 Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from treating a small console fan as more than it is.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Taking “fan included” to mean a built-in fan</h3>
              <p>Some listings put “Fan Included” in the title for a loose USB clip-on fan. If you want one built into the console, read the bullets for “built-in fan” or “cooling fan” on the console, or check the maker’s spec sheet.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing a weaker treadmill because it has a fan</h3>
              <p>A console fan is the cheapest part of the machine. If one treadmill has the better deck, motor or warranty and the other has a fan, buy the better treadmill and add a standalone fan, many of which cost under $100.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting the console fan to cool a hot room</h3>
              <p>A built-in fan moves a little of the room’s own air at your face. In a warm garage or closed spare room it will not keep you cool. Ventilate or cool the room first, then put a proper fan in front of the deck.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming a model has a fan because an older version did</h3>
              <p>Consoles change between model years, and listings are often reused. Confirm the fan on the current listing or maker page for the exact model you are buying, and check it when the treadmill arrives, inside the return window.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">The SOLE F65 is the best treadmill with a built-in fan for most
            people</strong>: a crossflow fan stated by both Amazon and SOLE, a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph,
            15% incline and a 350 lb rating for $1,199.99. On a tight budget, the THERUN 15% Auto Incline
            folder gets you a stated fan and powered incline for about $387; runners under $1,000 should buy
            the THERUN 20%; the SOLE F50 is the compact brand-name option; the Horizon 7.4 AT has the widest
            deck; and the ANCHEER and FUNMILY cover 450 lb users and streaming.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buy the treadmill for its deck, motor and warranty, and treat the fan as a welcome extra. A console
            fan keeps your face fresh on a walk; it does not cool your body on a hard run or cool a warm room.
            Check that the listing or maker names a built-in fan, test it in the return window, and if you run,
            put a real fan in front of the deck. Our <a href="/best-treadmill-fans/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best treadmill fans</a> guide covers those, and <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill Black Friday deals</a> tracks this week&rsquo;s prices.
          </p>
